WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.134 --> 00:00:18.984
Anne woke up feeling miserable. She had a bad toothache and had not slept all night.

2
00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:27.735
She left for school in a bad mood, and when she arrived, the classroom was cold and smoky because the fire wasn't burning well.

3
00:00:27.736 --> 00:00:32.050
The children were huddled around the stove, shivering.

4
00:00:32.051 --> 00:00:35.645
Anne sent her students to their desks.

5
00:00:35.646 --> 00:00:43.492
Later a student named Barbara Shaw came up to the blackboard to answer a math problem, and she tripped over the coal scuttle.

6
00:00:43.493 --> 00:00:46.471
Coal rolled to every part of the room.

7
00:00:46.472 --> 00:00:53.655
When Barbara stood up, her face was covered in coal dust and the boys roared with laughter.

8
00:00:53.656 --> 00:01:02.191
"Really, Barbara," Anne said icily, "if you cannot move without falling over something, you'd better remain in your seat."

9
00:01:02.192 --> 00:01:10.520
As poor Barbara went back to her desk, her tears combined with the coal dust, producing a truly grotesque effect on her cheeks.

10
00:01:10.521 --> 00:01:15.523
Then Anne caught Joe Sloane passing a small bag to Robert Brown.

11
00:01:15.524 --> 00:01:18.495
Anne guessed that the bag contained candy.

12
00:01:18.496 --> 00:01:23.428
She had warned her students before that candy in class would not be allowed.

13
00:01:23.429 --> 00:01:29.385
And yet now Joe Sloane was passing candy against her explicit instructions!

14
00:01:29.386 --> 00:01:36.435
"Joe," said Anne quietly, "bring that bag here." Startled, Joe obeyed.

15
00:01:36.436 --> 00:01:40.135
"Now throw it into the fire," said Anne.

16
00:01:40.136 --> 00:01:47.419
Joe looked very scared. "P-please, Miss," he stammered.

17
00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:51.018
"Do what I say, Joe," Anne said sternly.

18
00:01:51.019 --> 00:01:56.423
"B-but they . . . they're–" Joe said desperately.

19
00:01:56.424 --> 00:02:01.232
"Joe, are you going to obey me or not?" asked Anne.

20
00:02:01.233 --> 00:02:08.634
Finally, with an agonized glance at Robert Brown, Joe went to the fireplace and threw the bag in.

21
00:02:08.635 --> 00:02:17.516
For a few moments, Anne and the terrified students of Avonlea school did not know whether an earthquake or a volcano had hit them.

22
00:02:17.517 --> 00:02:25.362
The innocent-looking parcel, which Anne had thought contained candy, actually contained an assortment of firecrackers.

23
00:02:25.363 --> 00:02:30.930
The firecrackers exploded loudly, and sparks flew around the classroom.

24
00:02:30.931 --> 00:02:37.802
Anne sat down in her chair, pale, while all the girls climbed onto their desks shrieking.

25
00:02:37.803 --> 00:02:45.070
An hour later the fireworks were gone, and the room was tidied. Everyone was sitting quietly again.

26
00:02:45.071 --> 00:02:48.625
If Anne had been in a better mood, she might have laughed.

27
00:02:48.626 --> 00:02:52.900
Instead her mood had grown even worse.

28
00:02:52.901 --> 00:03:01.300
When Anne returned to the classroom after lunch, all the children were in their seats, studying, except for one—Anthony Pye.

29
00:03:01.301 --> 00:03:07.142
Anne opened her desk drawer to get some chalk, and a mouse leaped out and onto the floor.

30
00:03:07.143 --> 00:03:12.034
As Anne screamed and jumped, Anthony Pye laughed aloud.

31
00:03:12.035 --> 00:03:16.918
The mouse ran out the door, and the classroom was silent.

32
00:03:16.919 --> 00:03:21.312
"Who put that mouse in my desk?" asked Anne.

33
00:03:21.313 --> 00:03:27.106
Her voice was quite low, but it made a shiver go up and down Paul Irving's spine.

34
00:03:27.107 --> 00:03:33.376
She walked past Joe Sloane, who stuttered out wildly, "N-not m-me Teacher!"

35
00:03:33.377 --> 00:03:44.147
Anne paid no attention to Joe. She looked at Anthony Pye, and Anthony Pye looked back, unashamed. "Anthony, was it you?" asked Anne.

36
00:03:44.148 --> 00:03:48.063
"Yes, it was," he said insolently.

37
00:03:48.064 --> 00:03:50.600
Anne took her pointer from her desk.

38
00:03:50.601 --> 00:03:56.056
It was a long, heavy pointer. "Come here, Anthony."

39
00:03:56.057 --> 00:04:02.326
It was not the worst punishment Anthony had ever received, and Anne could never punish a child with cruelty.

40
00:04:02.327 --> 00:04:08.379
But the pointer did hurt a little. Anthony winced, and tears came to his eyes.

41
00:04:08.380 --> 00:04:16.322
Anne felt terrible, dropped the pointer, and told Anthony to go to his seat.

42
00:04:16.323 --> 00:04:23.559
Anne sat down at her desk. She felt ashamed and disappointed in herself for hitting a student.

43
00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:26.763
She had boasted that she would never do such a thing!

44
00:04:26.764 --> 00:04:33.225
How Jane Andrews would laugh at her! But worst of all, the students were looking at her with shock.

45
00:04:33.226 --> 00:04:36.215
And now Anthony Pye would never like her.

46
00:04:36.216 --> 00:04:40.091
She had tried hard to win his respect and love.

47
00:04:40.092 --> 00:04:44.496
That night at Green Gables, Anne cried in her bedroom with remorse.

48
00:04:44.497 --> 00:04:49.945
Marilla came in and exclaimed, "Why, Anne, what's the matter?"

49
00:04:49.946 --> 00:04:54.349
"Oh Marilla, I've had a terrible day," cried Anne.

50
00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:59.525
"At school today, I hit Anthony Pye for being naughty."

51
00:04:59.526 --> 00:05:02.779
"I'm glad to hear it," said Marilla.

52
00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:05.617
"You should have done it a long time ago!"

53
00:05:05.618 --> 00:05:12.802
"No, no, Marilla!" Anne cried. "You don't know how angry and mean I was.

54
00:05:12.803 --> 00:05:21.119
I can't forget the expression in Paul Irving's eyes. He looked so surprised and disappointed."

55
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:26.689
Marilla stroked Anne's hair and said, "Never mind. We all make mistakes.

56
00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:31.208
This day's done and tomorrow is a new day."

57
00:05:31.209 --> 00:05:37.281
The next morning on the way to school, Anthony Pye met Anne in the lane.

58
00:05:37.282 --> 00:05:40.809
"Can I carry those books for you, Teacher?" he asked.

59
00:05:40.810 --> 00:05:44.428
Anne blinked in surprise. Was she awake?

60
00:05:44.429 --> 00:05:50.836
At school, when Anne took her books back from Anthony, she smiled at him, and he smiled back.

61
00:05:50.837 --> 00:05:56.892
From that day on, Anthony Pye's behavior was excellent.

62
00:05:56.893 --> 00:06:03.584
Later Mrs. Lynde said Anthony Pye had told his mother that Anne had "hit him like a man."

63
00:06:03.585 --> 00:06:08.318
"I never expected to get his respect by hitting him," said Anne.

64
00:06:08.319 --> 00:06:14.903
"My theory about kindness—that it's better than punishing students—can't be wrong."

65
00:06:14.904 --> 00:06:27.840
"No, but the Pyes are an exception to every rule," declared Mrs. Lynde.
